{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "10-galois.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Galois connections and closure operators",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Find the best approximation of an element of one order in another, recognise the pattern as a Galois connection, and see the closure operator it makes; then build the concept lattice of a small formal context.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A ruler is marked only at the even lengths $0, 2, 4, 6$, read as $0, 1, 2, 3$. A length $x$ from $0$ to $6$ has to be reported as one reading $y$, and the reading must never understate it: $x \\le 2y$. Several readings are safe. The best is the least of them, $\\lceil x/2 \\rceil$.\n\n> [!try]\n> For which readings $y$ is $\\lceil x/2 \\rceil \\le y$? Compare them with the safe readings.\n\nThey are the same: $\\lceil x / 2 \\rceil \\le y$ exactly when $x \\le 2y$. That one equivalence says everything about $\\lceil x/2 \\rceil$: it is safe (take $y = \\lceil x/2 \\rceil$), and it is below every safe reading.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Galois connection\n> Monotone maps $f : P \\to Q$ and $g : Q \\to P$ form a **Galois connection** when $f(x) \\le y \\iff x \\le g(y)$ for all $x$ and $y$. Then $f(x)$ is the best approximation of $x$ in $Q$ from above, as $g$ judges it: the least $y$ with $x \\le g(y)$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let C7 = chain(7)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let C4 = chain(4)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let half = map(C7, C4; 0->0, 1->1, 2->1, 3->2, 4->2, 5->3, 6->3)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let dbl = map(C4, C7; 0->0, 1->2, 2->4, 3->6)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "galois(C7, C4, half, dbl)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!mistake]\n> The first guess at \"halve\" is often to round down. It understates: a length of $1$ would read $0$. The check names that pair.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let flo = map(C7, C4; 0->0, 1->0, 2->1, 3->1, 4->2, 5->2, 6->3)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "galois(C7, C4, flo, dbl)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Rounding down has its own job: it is the best approximation from *below*. The largest reading that never overstates $x$ is $\\lfloor x/2 \\rfloor$, since $2y \\le x \\iff y \\le \\lfloor x/2 \\rfloor$: doubling and rounding down form a Galois connection the other way round.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "galois(C4, C7, dbl, flo)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Round trips are closures",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Measure, then read back the length the reading stands for: $x \\mapsto 2\\lceil x/2 \\rceil$, which rounds up to an even length. It never goes down, it is monotone, and doing it twice is doing it once.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let even = map(C7; 1->2, 3->4, 5->6)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "closureop(C7, even)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fixpoints(C7, even)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Closure operator\n> A map $c$ on a poset is a **closure operator** when it is **extensive** ($x \\le c(x)$), **monotone**, and **idempotent** ($c(c(x)) = c(x)$). Its fixed points are the **closed** elements.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "For every Galois connection, $g \\circ f$ is a closure operator on $P$. Here the closed elements are the even lengths, the ones the ruler states exactly. Closure operators also turn up on their own. On the divisors of 12, sending $3$ up to $6$ is one; sending $2$ down to $1$ is not, since a closure never goes down:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let D = divisors(12)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let cl = map(D; 3->6)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "closureop(D, cl)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let bad = map(D; 2->1)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "closureop(D, bad)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Formal concept analysis",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Formal context, concept\n> A **context** lists objects, attributes, and which object has which. A **concept** is a set of objects and a set of attributes that determine each other: exactly the objects having all those attributes, and exactly the attributes they all share. Ordered by their objects, the concepts form a complete lattice.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let A = context({duck, eagle, dog, bat}, {flies, mammal, bird}; duck->flies, duck->bird, eagle->flies, eagle->bird, dog->mammal, bat->flies, bat->mammal)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "concepts(A)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The two maps \"the attributes these objects share\" and \"the objects having these attributes\" form a Galois connection (one that reverses the order: more objects share fewer attributes), and the concepts are its closed pairs. The bat sits below both \"flies\" and \"mammal\": the lattice finds the category nobody named.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "structure GaloisConnection {α β : Type} [PartialOrder α] [PartialOrder β] (f : α → β) (g : β → α) : Prop where\n  gc : ∀ (a : α) (b : β), f a ≤ b ↔ a ≤ g b",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem GaloisConnection.le_gf {α β : Type} [PartialOrder α] [PartialOrder β] {f : α → β} {g : β → α}\n    (h : GaloisConnection f g) (a : α) : a ≤ g (f a) := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove that $g \\circ f$ is extensive.",
      "leanSolution": "  exact (h.gc a (f a)).mp (le_refl (f a))",
      "hints": [
        "Use the connection with $b = f(a)$.",
        "`le_refl (f a)` proves `f a ≤ f a`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem GaloisConnection.fg_le {α β : Type} [PartialOrder α] [PartialOrder β] {f : α → β} {g : β → α}\n    (h : GaloisConnection f g) (b : β) : f (g b) ≤ b :=\n  (h.gc (g b) b).mpr (le_refl (g b))\n\ntheorem GaloisConnection.monotone_f {α β : Type} [PartialOrder α] [PartialOrder β] {f : α → β} {g : β → α}\n    (h : GaloisConnection f g) : Monotone f where\n  map_le a a' haa := (h.gc a (f a')).mpr (le_trans _ _ _ haa (h.le_gf a'))",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "galois(C4, C7, dbl, half)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Doubling is the lower partner of rounding down. Is it also the lower partner of rounding up: is `galois(C4, C7, dbl, half)` true? Answer `true` or `false`.",
      "hints": [
        "Try $x = 1$, $y = 1$: is $2 \\cdot 1 \\le 1$? Is $1 \\le \\lceil 1/2 \\rceil$?"
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "fixpoints(D, cl)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "List the closed elements of `cl`, the closure that sends $3$ to $6$.",
      "hints": [
        "Every element `cl` does not move."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> A Galois connection pairs each element with its best approximation in another order, $f(x) \\le y \\iff x \\le g(y)$; composing the two maps gives a closure operator, which adds what is forced and then stops. Formal concept analysis is a Galois connection between objects and attributes, and its closed pairs are a lattice of concepts.\n\nComputing a closure in practice means applying a step until it adds nothing. Next: that loop, and why it stops.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
